WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.167 --> 00:00:15.669
Daisy and Demi were the apple of everyone's eye as they charmed whoever caught sight of them.

2
00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:21.733
Meg brought over the babies almost every day, knowing that their adorable faces would bring smiles to a

3
00:00:21.793 --> 00:00:22.593
mourning household.

4
00:00:22.613 --> 00:00:26.856
"Our Daisy is a stubborn one," John said proudly to Mother and Father.

5
00:00:27.804 --> 00:00:31.746
"If her brother comes near her space, she kicks and screams until he moves away."

6
00:00:31.766 --> 00:00:36.609
"Sounds like our Jo when she was a baby," Mother said with a wink.

7
00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:38.770
Everyone laughed at the resemblance.

8
00:00:39.411 --> 00:00:42.332
It was true the twins could not have been more opposite in temperament.

9
00:00:42.973 --> 00:00:48.076
Demi, the boy, was mild, quiet, and obedient, while Daisy threw tantrums until she got her way.

10
00:00:48.776 --> 00:00:52.618
But both babies looked like perfect angels when they slept in their father's arms.

11
00:00:52.638 --> 00:00:55.280
"Brings back memories, doesn't it, dear?"

12
00:00:55.532 --> 00:00:57.933
Father said tenderly, putting his arm around his wife.

13
00:00:58.854 --> 00:01:03.156
Jo watched as Meg placed a loving hand on her husband's arm and leaned her head on his

14
00:01:03.196 --> 00:01:05.518
shoulder, while their babies slept peacefully.

15
00:01:06.158 --> 00:01:11.101
Instead of being moved by seeing such a loving family, it made Jo feel tremendously lonely and sad.

16
00:01:12.221 --> 00:01:16.363
Making her excuses, she went upstairs, got her notebook, and walked out to the pond.

17
00:01:17.764 --> 00:01:23.227
It was no fault of theirs, but Jo felt distanced from her parents, from Meg and John, and

18
00:01:23.287 --> 00:01:26.441
also from Amy and Laurie, knowing they were seeing Europe together.

19
00:01:27.121 --> 00:01:31.804
It wasn't jealousy, but Jo felt as though everyone had someone special to spend time with except for

20
00:01:31.864 --> 00:01:36.246
her. "This is ridiculous," she said to her reflection in the water.

21
00:01:36.266 --> 00:01:38.628
"I've done well on my own till now.

22
00:01:39.268 --> 00:01:41.629
Why should I start feeling sorry for myself?"

23
00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:47.893
No matter how many times she told herself that, however, Jo still felt the empty space in her

24
00:01:47.913 --> 00:01:49.273
heart gradually growing bigger.

25
00:01:50.194 --> 00:01:55.361
Sighing loudly, she opened the notebook the Professor had given her and began writing, escaping into the world

26
00:01:55.421 --> 00:01:57.743
of make-believe where no one suffered loneliness.

27
00:01:59.804 --> 00:02:05.108
In Florence, Amy sat on the balcony of the villa, silent tears falling onto her beautiful dress.

28
00:02:05.888 --> 00:02:09.211
In her hand was the telegram from home telling her of Beth's passing.

29
00:02:09.971 --> 00:02:14.515
She had known that Beth was ill and that eventually this day would come, but Amy thought she

30
00:02:14.555 --> 00:02:16.196
would be with her family when it happened.

31
00:02:16.976 --> 00:02:21.239
Now she felt as if she had betrayed Beth for not being with her during the last months

32
00:02:21.279 --> 00:02:22.060
of her life on earth.

33
00:02:23.452 --> 00:02:25.653
"I'm so selfish for being here.

34
00:02:25.673 --> 00:02:30.456
I should be with my family," she cried to Aunt March when she first received the telegram.

35
00:02:30.476 --> 00:02:34.519
"Beth wished for you to stay here, so you shall.

36
00:02:35.139 --> 00:02:40.262
We must let go of the dying and hold fast to life and live," Aunt March said bluntly.

37
00:02:41.122 --> 00:02:45.025
Clearly, if Amy wanted any comfort, she wasn't going to get it from Aunt March.

38
00:02:46.145 --> 00:02:50.248
For days Amy did not eat, speak, or do anything but sit on the balcony and cry.

39
00:02:51.108 --> 00:02:54.931
One day while she was doing just that, clinging to the piece of paper from home as if

40
00:02:55.011 --> 00:02:57.674
it was her only link to her family, she prayed for Beth.

41
00:02:58.654 --> 00:03:02.097
When Amy opened her eyes, she couldn't believe who was walking toward the villa.

42
00:03:02.117 --> 00:03:05.420
"Laurie?" she cried in a little girl's voice.

43
00:03:06.761 --> 00:03:11.185
Amy could see Laurie's distressed face looking up at her and she knew he had also heard about

44
00:03:11.205 --> 00:03:16.289
Beth. She ran downstairs and flung herself into his arms as soon as she opened the door.

45
00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.052
They stood there like that, holding each other and crying together.

46
00:03:21.012 --> 00:03:23.254
"I'm so sorry, Amy," Laurie kept whispering.

47
00:03:23.274 --> 00:03:25.717
"We all loved her very much."

48
00:03:27.378 --> 00:03:32.584
Nodding but unable to answer, Amy kept crying, yet she found a calming comfort in Laurie's arms.

49
00:03:33.925 --> 00:03:39.330
On a rainy afternoon too miserable to venture outside to the pond where Jo had been doing most

50
00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:44.155
of her recent writing, she decided, for some odd reason, to pay a visit to Mr.

51
00:03:45.676 --> 00:03:50.900
Laurence. Without question Beth had been his favorite girl, so her death had affected Mr.

52
00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:51.840
Laurence deeply.

53
00:03:52.701 --> 00:03:58.865
Because he wasn't family, however, Jo failed to realize just how much the old man must have been

54
00:03:58.905 --> 00:03:59.366
missing her.

55
00:04:00.206 --> 00:04:02.688
She thought it was a good time to see how Mr.

56
00:04:02.728 --> 00:04:03.729
Laurence was coping.

57
00:04:03.749 --> 00:04:10.273
"Jo! What a pleasant surprise, my dear," he said happily when he saw who had come to visit.

58
00:04:10.934 --> 00:04:13.896
Jo had baked some oatmeal cookies, which she handed over with a smile.

59
00:04:14.372 --> 00:04:17.613
"I'm sorry I haven't been here sooner," she apologized.

60
00:04:18.773 --> 00:04:19.974
Holding up his hand, Mr.

61
00:04:19.994 --> 00:04:24.155
Laurence stopped her, saying, "Jo, under the circumstances, it's understandable.

62
00:04:24.855 --> 00:04:28.696
But I expect you to come and visit this lonely old man at least once a day from

63
00:04:28.736 --> 00:04:29.276
now on."

64
00:04:29.296 --> 00:04:35.258
"I will, and since I'm a lonely old woman we can be lonely together," she said miserably.

65
00:04:35.278 --> 00:04:37.939
"Splendid!" Mr.

66
00:04:37.959 --> 00:04:41.000
Laurence roared as if he hadn't laughed out loud in a while.

67
00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.622
They talked about the babies, Amy and Laurie, and other things.

68
00:04:45.203 --> 00:04:45.723
Then Mr.

69
00:04:45.763 --> 00:04:47.765
Laurence said something very interesting.

70
00:04:47.785 --> 00:04:53.029
"You know, Jo, I'd like to do something to commemorate Beth's life," he said.

71
00:04:53.049 --> 00:04:54.771
"What were you thinking of?"

72
00:04:55.151 --> 00:04:55.591
asked Jo.

73
00:04:55.611 --> 00:05:01.636
"Some kind of place that would bring good to the community, just as Beth always did kind acts

74
00:05:01.696 --> 00:05:03.138
for others," he explained.

75
00:05:03.158 --> 00:05:05.199
"What do you think Beth would like?"

76
00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:13.129
Jo thought for a moment, then said, "Well, Beth always loved children, so perhaps something for little ones?"

77
00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:16.771
Mr. Laurence stood up and began pacing the room.

78
00:05:17.711 --> 00:05:21.413
He said, "Yes, yes, a school for children would be a fine idea.

79
00:05:21.853 --> 00:05:22.633
A piano school?"

80
00:05:23.794 --> 00:05:29.336
Jo nodded in agreement and said, "Beth loved music so that would be a wonderful memorial to her."

81
00:05:30.337 --> 00:05:35.879
Mr. Laurence stopped suddenly and said, "True, but Beth would want children to learn so much more.

82
00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:41.481
Jo! What about a school that teaches academics and arts such as piano or painting?"

83
00:05:42.681 --> 00:05:46.362
Jo sat up straight, remembering someone else who wanted to start a school.

84
00:05:46.382 --> 00:05:52.544
"Mr. Laurence, if you do open a school in Beth's name, I already know the perfect person to

85
00:05:52.584 --> 00:05:54.845
be the headmaster," she said excitedly.

86
00:05:55.745 --> 00:05:57.265
Grabbing her hands tightly, Mr.

87
00:05:57.325 --> 00:06:00.426
Laurence said emotionally, "Let's make this work, Jo.

88
00:06:00.786 --> 00:06:02.247
Let's do this for Beth."

89
00:06:03.767 --> 00:06:08.112
Letting go of his hands and giving him a tight hug instead, Jo cried, "Thank you, Mr.

90
00:06:08.152 --> 00:06:12.974
Laurence. This means so much to us, and Beth would be so proud if she knew what you

91
00:06:13.014 --> 00:06:13.854
were doing for her."

92
00:06:15.455 --> 00:06:20.076
That evening, Jo wrote a long, detailed letter to the Professor, telling all that had happened in the

93
00:06:20.096 --> 00:06:21.657
past few months and ending with Mr.

94
00:06:21.677 --> 00:06:22.637
Laurence's proposal.

95
00:06:22.657 --> 00:06:28.779
"If you are interested in this position, you may wish to make a trip here and meet Mr.

96
00:06:28.819 --> 00:06:32.080
Laurence in person to discuss the details further," she wrote.

97
00:06:33.152 --> 00:06:37.816
Placing the letter in an envelope and sealing it, Jo said, "Now, we'll just have to wait and

98
00:06:37.916 --> 00:06:38.816
see what happens."

99
00:06:40.318 --> 00:06:44.661
Just a few days later, that same envelope was handed over to the Professor, who read the letter

100
00:06:44.681 --> 00:06:45.542
with much interest.

101
00:06:46.422 --> 00:06:50.726
"Hmm, this sounds very promising indeed and highly tempting.

102
00:06:51.526 --> 00:06:53.648
I must find out about a coach ticket."
